WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.725
Undefined behavior means the C standard
does not define what the program must do for

2
00:00:07.805 --> 00:00:10.076
a construct or situation.

3
00:00:10.156 --> 00:00:13.459
Once code enters undefined behavior,

4
00:00:13.539 --> 00:00:20.455
the compiler and platform are not required
to preserve the behavior a developer

5
00:00:20.535 --> 00:00:23.218
expected from a local test run.

6
00:00:23.298 --> 00:00:30.111
Compiler optimizations can make undefined
behavior especially surprising.

7
00:00:30.191 --> 00:00:37.313
An optimization may assume that certain
invalid states never occur and transform

8
00:00:37.393 --> 00:00:40.077
code based on that assumption.

9
00:00:40.157 --> 00:00:46.143
A program can appear to work under one
compiler, optimization level,

10
00:00:46.223 --> 00:00:50.868
or architecture and fail differently under
another.

11
00:00:50.948 --> 00:00:57.554
Common areas to review include
out-of-bounds access, invalid pointer use,

12
00:00:57.634 --> 00:01:03.311
signed integer overflow, uninitialized
reads, invalid shifts,

13
00:01:03.391 --> 00:01:08.346
strict aliasing assumptions, and lifetime
violations.

14
00:01:08.426 --> 00:01:11.729
These are not only academic concerns.

15
00:01:11.809 --> 00:01:18.931
They affect whether the compiled program
still matches the programmer's intent.

16
00:01:19.011 --> 00:01:25.617
Security-sensitive code should avoid
relying on undefined, unspecified,

17
00:01:25.697 --> 00:01:29.206
or implementation-specific behavior.

18
00:01:29.286 --> 00:01:36.305
If behavior depends on compiler flags,
platform representation, integer size,

19
00:01:36.385 --> 00:01:39.791
byte order, or alignment assumptions,

20
00:01:39.871 --> 00:01:46.581
document and test those assumptions rather
than letting them remain hidden.

21
00:01:46.661 --> 00:01:53.060
Compiler warnings and sanitizers can help
identify undefined behavior,

22
00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:56.340
but they do not prove all code is safe.

23
00:01:56.420 --> 00:02:00.342
They are part of the review and test
strategy.

24
00:02:00.422 --> 00:02:06.409
They work best when teams run them
regularly, investigate findings,

25
00:02:06.489 --> 00:02:11.959
and keep the codebase clean enough that
new warnings stand out.

26
00:02:12.039 --> 00:02:17.717
Portability matters when code is built for
different compilers,

27
00:02:17.797 --> 00:02:24.815
optimization levels, architectures,
operating systems, or embedded targets.

28
00:02:24.895 --> 00:02:31.914
A secure C baseline treats compiler
assumptions as part of the design and release

29
00:02:31.994 --> 00:02:32.820
process.
